      "math_academy_id": "q-48283",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "The function $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k})=\\sqrt{k}.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =f(0) \\\\ \\sqrt{k} & =2 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
